{{Infobox|title=High-bandwidth memory}}
<onlyinclude>'''High-bandwidth memory''' ('''HBM''') is a family of stacked [[Dynamic random-access memory|DRAM]] technologies designed to supply high aggregate data bandwidth through wide interfaces and advanced package-level connections to processors. It is used in high-performance computing and accelerator systems, where data movement can constrain throughput.</onlyinclude>

== Architecture ==
HBM stacks multiple DRAM dies and uses through-silicon vias (TSVs) and associated interconnect structures to connect layers. A memory controller communicates with a wide, partitioned interface. HBM is a form of DRAM, not a replacement for processor caches or a guarantee of low access latency.

== System integration ==
HBM is integrated close to processing dies using advanced packaging. Package routing, thermals, capacity, controller scheduling, and memory-access locality affect realized performance. The bandwidth of one stack should not be confused with the aggregate bandwidth of a multi-stack processor.

